import json, glob, os

BASE = "/home/hatch/workspace/apex-audit/docs/university/data/courses/stories"

CODAS = {
"il-01-esoteric-foundations-story-2": '''A final detail worth carrying into the module: Dee's diaries show the sessions were collaborative and contested — Kelley sometimes refused to scry, Dee sometimes doubted what came through, and the "angels" themselves set conditions. Whatever you believe crossed the crystal, the record shows two men negotiating with the unknown for seven years, in three countries, with a queen's favor on the line.''',
"il-01-esoteric-foundations-story-3": '''Wiener would have recognized the pattern instantly: a signal leaves the system, the environment reacts, the reaction returns as input. The CCRU simply noticed that the signal could be a story and the environment could be a market, a subculture, a timeline. Cybernetics began by steering anti-aircraft guns; it ends, for our purposes, by asking who steers the steersman when the feedback loop is made of belief.''',
"il-02-mathematical-maturity-engine-story-2": '''The method's first published form came in 1949, when Metropolis and Ulam formalized it — and Nicholas Metropolis would soon give the world the Metropolis algorithm, Monte Carlo's most famous child. Keep the lineage straight when you use it in quant work: simulation doesn't replace mathematics, it replaces the mathematics you cannot do.''',
"il-02-mathematical-maturity-engine-story-3": '''The resistance is the point worth remembering: Florence's bankers banned the new numerals partly because zero made fraud trivially easy, and scholars distrusted a symbol for nothing. Every powerful abstraction meets this reception first. Trade math won anyway — because the merchants who adopted place value could calculate interest, divide profits, and price risk while their competitors were still pushing beads.''',
"il-03-programming-language-forge-story-1": '''The Navy, to its credit, eventually listened: Hopper rose to rear admiral, and the establishment that once dismissed her compiler ended up running on her languages. Her deeper vindication is structural — every layer of abstraction you will build in this course, from assemblers to type systems to the frameworks that compile beneath your notice, is the A-0 idea compounded. Write for humans; let the machine translate.''',
"il-03-programming-language-forge-story-2": '''There is a coda the folklore usually omits: the Mark II's operators logged the moth, removed it, and kept working — the first recorded incident response in computing history, complete with a ticket, a root cause, and a resolution. Your debugging in this module follows the same ritual, minus the tweezers.''',
"il-03-programming-language-forge-story-3": '''The language's governance became part of the story too: van Rossum's "Benevolent Dictator For Life" model held for nearly three decades until he stepped down in 2018, handing Python to a steering council. The community he seeded — the package index, the conferences, the culture of readability — is as much his creation as the syntax. You are joining that community in this course, not just learning its grammar.''',
"il-04-systems-linux-windows-networks-story-2": '''The "ether" in Ethernet was itself a joke about dead physics — the luminiferous ether had been disproven decades earlier, but Metcalfe liked the image of bits radiating through a common medium. The medium changed from coaxial cable to twisted pair to fiber, the speed from 3 megabits to 400 gigabits, but the frame format's bones are still 1973. Learn the frame and you can read any of it.''',
"il-04-systems-linux-windows-networks-story-3": '''Kleinrock kept the lab notebooks, and the IMP log for that night survives — "talked to SRI host to host" scrawled in the margin of history. Within two years the network had email; within a decade, TCP/IP; within a quarter century, the web. Every protocol you'll configure in this module is a refinement of the bet those four nodes made: that a network which expects failure will outlive one that pretends it won't happen.''',
"il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-story-1": '''The mandate's most quoted line — "anyone who doesn't do this will be fired" — may be apocryphal in its exact wording, but the organizational result is documented: Amazon became a company of small, autonomous service teams years before microservices had a name. When you design a VPC in this module, you're practicing the same discipline at a smaller scale: define the interface, hide the implementation, and let other teams build on your contract.''',
"il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-story-2": '''The fifty-percent toil cap is the detail most teams skip and most regret skipping: Treynor's rule was that no SRE should spend more than half their time on operational toil, with the excess automatically converted into engineering projects. Toil that can't be eliminated gets automated; toil that can't be automated gets a project. It's the difference between a team that operates a system and a team the system operates.''',
"il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-story-3": '''The name was almost "Seven of Nine" — a Star Trek joke about Borg drones, with seven spokes in the original logo. Google chose the Greek instead, but the lineage is right there in the joke: Kubernetes is Borg, domesticated and donated. The CNCF, founded to hold it in 2015, became the home of the entire cloud-native ecosystem — Prometheus, Envoy, and everything else you'll meet in this program.''',
"il-06-cybersecurity-secure-engineering-story-2": '''The Soviet Union learned the price the hard way: reused one-time-pad keys in the 1940s let American cryptanalysts break into KGB traffic in the Venona project — proof that the pad's perfection evaporates the instant the discipline slips. Perfect secrecy is a property of the procedure, not the paper. That's why this module teaches key management as the main event, not the footnote.''',
"il-06-cybersecurity-secure-engineering-story-3": '''Kerckhoffs wrote for an audience of artillery officers, and his six principles included practical ones — the system should be easy to use, the key should be memorable and changeable. The second principle survived because it aligned incentives: a public algorithm gets attacked by everyone, including its friends, and what survives is trustworthy. Security through obscurity, by contrast, gets attacked only by its enemies — and you hear about it last.''',
"il-06-cybersecurity-secure-engineering-story-4": '''The graduate student at the center of it became a case study in second acts: Morris went on to co-found Viaweb, become a partner at Y Combinator, and take a professorship at MIT. The internet forgave him; the lesson stuck. And the worm's code had one more legacy — its techniques were studied so thoroughly that they became the curriculum. You're studying them now.''',
"il-07-data-engineering-analytics-ai-story-1": '''Google's original paper is remarkably candid about the failures the model absorbed — crashed workers, stragglers, corrupted disks — because at that scale, failure isn't an exception, it's the weather. That attitude is the real inheritance: design for the weather. Spark, born at Berkeley's AMPLab in 2009, kept the two-verb soul and added memory; the cloud warehouses kept the scale and hid the verbs entirely.''',
"il-07-data-engineering-analytics-ai-story-2": '''The "random surfer" was more than a metaphor — it made the web's importance mathematically precise as the stationary distribution of a Markov chain, the same mathematics behind the statistics in this program. And the founders' garage is now legend: Google's first servers were corkboard and cheap disks in a Menlo Park garage rented in September 1998. Structure, statistics, and scrappiness — the whole company in three words.''',
"il-07-data-engineering-analytics-ai-story-3": '''Pitts's own story is the field's great tragedy: the brilliant runaway logician suffered a breakdown, burned his thesis, and died young in 1969 — the same year "Perceptrons" froze the field he helped create. The neuron survived its creators' quarrels. When your model converges in this course, spare a thought for the teenager who walked to Chicago with nothing but mathematics.''',
"il-08-algorithms-compilers-distributed-formal-story-1": '''Hoare published the algorithm when he was 27, and he has spent the decades since trying to make software correct — his "billion-dollar mistake" was the null reference, invented because it was easy and regretted ever since. The pairing is instructive: the same mind that gave us the fastest sort gave us the costliest bug. Speed without proof is a loan; this module teaches you to check the interest rate.''',
"il-08-algorithms-compilers-distributed-formal-story-2": '''Dijkstra became famous as much for his discipline as his algorithms — his handwritten EWD manuscripts, over 1,300 of them, argued that programming should be a mathematical activity, done carefully or not at all. The café story survives because it captures the truth he preached: the best algorithms feel discovered, not constructed.''',
"il-08-algorithms-compilers-distributed-formal-story-3": '''Cook's paper was nearly rejected — the program committee didn't see the point, and only its persistence in the proceedings let Karp build on it a year later. The deepest question in the field survived by accident. And the million dollars is still on the table: the Clay Institute's prize has sat unclaimed for over two decades, while every cryptosystem you use bets its life on the answer.''',
"il-09-adaptive-certification-command-story-1": '''The Times headline in the genesis block did double duty: it timestamped the chain, proving it began after January 3, 2009, and it indicted the system Bitcoin was built to bypass. Nakamoto never spent the roughly one million coins mined in those early days — one of the great unclaimed fortunes in history, sitting motionless as a monument to the idea that the money was never the point.''',
"il-09-adaptive-certification-command-story-2": '''Szabo's vending-machine analogy was deliberately humble — he wanted readers to see that self-enforcing agreements were already everywhere, just waiting to be generalized. The generalization took twenty years and a blockchain. When you write your first contract in this module, notice how much of Szabo's 1994 vocabulary still fits: the clauses, the enforcement, the removal of the trusted third party.''',
"il-09-adaptive-certification-command-story-3": '''Codd's revenge on IBM's hesitation was complete: the company eventually shipped DB2 and became a relational giant, but only after Oracle had defined the market. And Boyce, the co-creator of SEQUEL, died in 1974 at 27 — the same year his language was published — so he never saw what it became. The normal forms you memorize for the exam are his and Codd's mathematics, still enforced by every query planner you'll meet.''',
"il-09-adaptive-certification-command-story-4": '''The Green Team's office had no sign and no status — a skunkworks inside a workstation company, building for a market that didn't exist. Gosling has said the team figured the project would be cancelled within months. Instead the web arrived like a rescue ship, and the language designed for an unshippable TV box became the enterprise standard. Build for the constraints in front of you; the platform may outlive the plan.''',
"il-10-portfolio-career-professional-practice-story-1": '''The cold start was brutal: for months the site grew one invitation at a time, and the founders seeded it with their own networks. Hoffman's insight was that professionals would pay in data for access — to recruiters, to opportunities, to each other — and the exchange needed a trusted ledger. Your profile is that ledger entry. In this module you'll learn to make it the kind a hiring manager can't ignore.''',
"il-10-portfolio-career-professional-practice-story-2": '''Blank's contribution is often under-credited: his "Four Steps to the Epiphany" (2005) argued that startups search while companies execute — a distinction Ries turned into the build-measure-learn loop. The method has since been formalized, taught, and occasionally cargo-culted; the cargo-cult version skips the hard part, which is the learning. Measure honestly, or the loop is theater.''',
"il-10-portfolio-career-professional-practice-story-3": '''Von Neumann's minimax theorem — the proof that every finite game has a rational solution — was published in 1928, sixteen years before the book, and it came from thinking about parlor games. Schelling won his Nobel in 2005 for showing the same mathematics governs nuclear standoffs and neighborhood segregation. The quant's edge is seeing the game inside the market: everyone else sees prices; you see strategies.''',
"il-11-zero-trust-endpoint-defense-story-1": '''The federal mandate gave the obituary teeth: Executive Order 14028 (2021) ordered agencies toward zero-trust architectures on a deadline, and the CISA maturity model turned Kindervag's philosophy into a scored rubric. What began as a Forrester report became procurement policy. When you implement these controls in this course, you're building to a standard that now moves billions in federal spending.''',
"il-11-zero-trust-endpoint-defense-story-2": '''The malware was a Citadel variant, and it scraped memory from the registers in real time — the industry's wake-up call to encrypt data even in RAM. Target's breach also rewrote the vendor-management playbook: third-party risk, network segmentation for vendors, and the principle that a contractor's laptop is part of your attack surface. Every vendor-access review you'll perform descends from Fazio Mechanical's phished inbox.''',
"il-11-zero-trust-endpoint-defense-story-3": '''The protocol's age is its credential: Kerberos has been attacked by the world's best for nearly forty years and the core design stands — the breaks have all been in implementations, configurations, and the humans around them. That's why this module teaches both sides: the mathematics deserves your respect, and the ticket forgeries deserve your fluency. The three-headed dog still guards the gate.''',
"il-12-ai-engineering-story-1": '''The 1986 paper's most quoted promise was that networks would "learn their own representations" — and the decades since have been the fulfillment of that promise, from edges to objects to language itself. Hinton, who kept the faith through two AI winters, shared the 2018 Turing Award for it. When your loss curve bends downward in this module, you're watching 1986 happen again, one gradient at a time.''',
"il-12-ai-engineering-story-2": '''The dataset mattered as much as the network: Fei-Fei Li's ImageNet, built by paying thousands of workers to label millions of images, was the fuel without which the architecture couldn't have proven itself. Data, compute, algorithm — AlexNet was the first proof that the three multiply. Every training run you'll launch in this course is that same multiplication, and the discipline is in managing all three at once.''',
"il-12-ai-engineering-story-3": '''The paper's eight authors scattered to found or lead much of the industry you now inhabit — startups, labs, and the next architectures. None of them, by their own accounts, predicted the scale of what followed; they were trying to translate German better. The lesson for the AI engineer: the most consequential work often looks like a narrow engineering improvement. Build the better translation system; the era may follow.''',
"il-12-ai-engineering-story-4": '''Weizenbaum's secretary has become the story's emblem — the insider, who knew exactly how the trick worked, asking for privacy with the machine. The ELIZA effect doesn't spare the knowledgeable; it exploits the human, not the ignorant. Your users will confide in your systems too. Design the prompts, the disclosures, and the boundaries with her in mind — she knew it was pattern matching, and she asked him to leave the room anyway.''',
"il-13-software-engineering-interview-prep-story-1": '''Fielding has spent years correcting the industry's misuse of his term — most "REST APIs" violate the hypermedia constraint he considered essential, and he has said so publicly, dryly. The irony is instructive for interviews: the test isn't whether you can recite the dissertation, it's whether you understand which constraints buy you scale. Statelessness buys horizontal scaling; cacheability buys the CDN; the uniform interface buys evolvability. Know the price of each.''',
"il-13-software-engineering-interview-prep-story-2": '''Eich went on to co-found Mozilla and later Brave, but his ten days in May remain the most consequential rush job in software history. The language's survival is a case study in the power of distribution over design: the best language doesn't win, the one that's everywhere does — and then, given twenty years and V8, the everywhere language gets good. Your frontend interviews test the scars of that history; wear them as knowledge.''',
"il-13-software-engineering-interview-prep-story-3": '''Ritchie died in 2011, the same week as Steve Jobs — his passing was famously overshadowed in the press, a fact the programming world noted with some bitterness. The memorial the community built was more fitting: every kernel, every embedded device, every "hello, world" still compiles. The interview questions about undefined behavior are, in the end, questions about whether you respect the machine he gave you.''',
"il-14-sre-devops-cloud-security-interview-prep-story-1": '''The dashboard failure became its own lesson, quoted ever since: the status page depended on the system whose status it reported, so during the outage it showed everything as healthy. Amazon fixed the dependency and the industry adopted the principle — your observability must survive the failure it's meant to observe. When you design monitoring in this course, ask the dashboard question first: what does this show when the thing it watches is gone?''',
"il-14-sre-devops-cloud-security-interview-prep-story-2": '''The final ignominy was operational: Knight's engineers couldn't simply turn the system off, because the runaway code was spread across servers and the kill procedure wasn't clear — 45 minutes of helpless watching. The postmortem's quietest recommendation was the most important: know how to stop your own system. Every runbook you write in this course should begin there — the big red button, tested, before the feature ships.''',
"il-14-sre-devops-cloud-security-interview-prep-story-3": '''Denning later became a prominent voice in the crypto wars of the 1990s, testifying on key escrow and civil liberties — the detection pioneer turned privacy advocate, a reminder that the security field's founders often understood the stakes better than its vendors. Her 1987 model assumed what the industry keeps relearning: the attacker innovates, so the defense must model the normal. The signatures catch yesterday; the baselines catch tomorrow.''',
"il-15-data-engineering-analytics-interview-prep-story-1": '''The language's declarative nature is why it survived every challenger: you describe the result, and fifty years of optimizer research figures out the how. That division of labor — human intent, machine execution — is the same idea as Hopper's compiler, applied to data. In this module you'll learn to think declaratively at scale: say what you want precisely, and let the engine earn its keep.''',
"il-15-data-engineering-analytics-interview-prep-story-2": '''The lakehouse — the architecture you'll actually build on — is the synthesis made concrete: cheap object storage holding everything (Inmon's enterprise memory), with dimensional models and governed marts on top (Kimball's analyst-friendly shapes). The war ended not with a winner but with a stack. Your interview answer should show you know both ancestors, because the modern platform is their child.''',
"il-15-data-engineering-analytics-interview-prep-story-3": '''Fisher's own career carries the field's warning label: the father of experimental design was also a committed eugenicist, and his statistical tools were forged partly in service of that ideology. The methods are sound; the history is not innocent. It's worth knowing as you wield them — the mathematics of evidence deserves practitioners who understand both its power and its provenance.''',
}

updated = 0
for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(BASE, "il-*.json"))):
    if f.endswith("_index.json"):
        continue
    d = json.load(open(f))
    changed = False
    for s in d["stories"]:
        coda = CODAS.get(s["id"])
        if coda:
            s["narrative"] = s["narrative"].rstrip() + " " + coda
            changed = True
            updated += 1
    if changed:
        json.dump(d, open(f, "w"), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)

print("codas applied:", updated)

bad = []
for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(BASE, "il-*.json"))):
    if f.endswith("_index.json"):
        continue
    d = json.load(open(f))
    for s in d["stories"]:
        w = len(s["narrative"].split())
        if not (300 <= w <= 600):
            bad.append((s["id"], w))
print("still out of range:", bad if bad else "none")
